  "title": "Remove Nth Node From End of List",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": ["linked list", "two pointers"],
  "asked": "very_common",
  "statement": "You're given the head of a singly linked list and an integer `n`. Remove the n-th node counted from the end of the list (so `n = 1` is the last node) and return the head of the resulting list.\n\n`n` is always between 1 and the length of the list, so there is always a node to remove. Each node is a `ListNode` with a `val` and a `next` attribute; the class is already defined for you. In the examples, a list is written as its values from head to tail. Try to do it in a single pass over the list.\n\nConstraints: 1 to 500 nodes; -100 <= val <= 100; 1 <= n <= the length of the list.",
  "kind": "function",
  "functionName": "remove_nth_from_end",
  "signature": "def remove_nth_from_end(head: Optional[ListNode], n: int) -> Optional[ListNode]:",
  "starterCode": "def remove_nth_from_end(head: Optional[ListNode], n: int) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n    \"\"\"Remove the n-th node from the end and return the head of the list.\"\"\"\n    pass\n",
